  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Patchy fog this morning, with the potential for \nfrost and freezing conditions west of the Blue Ridge tonight \nthrough Tuesday night.\n\nPatchy fog has been observed mainly west of the metros at this\ntime. Guidance continues to be bullish on fog development\nthrough daybreak before the front arrives. Right now the fog is\npatchy so no headlines have been issued, but will. \n\nAny lingering cloud cover should erode as a cold front sweeps \nthrough the area early this morning giving way to sun. NW winds\npick up behind the front with afternoon gusts to around 20 to \n25 mph. A strong area of Canadian high pressure pushes in from \nthe Midwest this afternoon before settling over the local area \ntonight. This sets the stages for the first widespread frost \nevent, mainly west of the Blue Ridge. High elevation valleys \ncould even near the freezing mark. For east of the Blue Ridge, \nforecast lows will be in the 40s.\n\nHigh pressure maintains its influence over the area on Tuesday \nwhich brings cool temps, lighter winds, and sunny skies. \nTue night also looks chilly, but fcst temps have come up a tad \ngiven light sly flow around the departing high. At this point \ntemps would be just mild enough to avoid another frost event. \nFor most, the fcst lows are in the low/mid 40s.\n\nRemaining dry Wed with high pressure continuing to move east\nyielding a S'ly return flow. Temps Wed into the 70s for most,\ncooler in the mtns.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry finish to the work week before rain chances\nreturn late in the weekend.\n\nThe northern branch of the jet stream remains amplified throughout \nthe mid/late week period. One embedded sw will drive another \ndry cold front through the region on Thu before another round \nof high pressure returns by Fri and into portions of next \nweekend. This maintains the dry weather pattern through at least\nSat. Initial high temps start off in the mid/upper 70s on \nThu before falling back into the 60s to low 70s for the \nweekend. Overnight conditions remain seasonable without any \nthreat of frost or freeze at this time.\n\nThe pattern late in the weekend becomes more interesting as a \ndisturbance across the Gulf of America lifts nwrd toward the \nGulf Coast region. Depending on what remains of this feature and\nthe quality of the moisture, an uptick in rainfall chances can \nbe expected for Sun. The latest forecast package calls for a 30\nto 50 percent chance of rain, but high uncertainty.",
